{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794243805,"nonce":"uWikuBg+TwF73LpUz9Be7wIJImM=","title":"Windowed mouse-move short-circuit misses a sibling drawn over the last leaf","message":"`EventRouter::cursor_inside_last_leaf` lets the desktop runner skip hit\ntesting while the pointer stays inside the last deepest hit node, checking\nonly that node's direct children. A later sibling that overlaps the leaf (an\nabsolute decoration with an interactive element drawn above it) is not\nchecked, so moving from the leaf onto that sibling never hovers it until the\npointer leaves the leaf's bounds.\n\nSeen with cn tabs: the active trigger's indicator pill (absolute, inset 0, no\nchildren) was the leaf when the pointer entered through the trigger's padding;\nmoving onto the closable tab's close button, a later sibling over the pill,\nkept the pill hovered, so the button's :hover look came and went with the\npath the pointer took. Worked around by making the pill pointer-events: none.\n\nHeadless does not take this short-circuit, so recorder tests cannot see it.\n\nThe same handler's 8 ms coalescing gate drops a move without replaying it, so\na pointer that stops within 8 ms of the previous dispatched move keeps a stale\nhover until it moves again. The comment there assumes a later event always\nfollows.\n\nFix: have the short-circuit also check nodes painted above the leaf whose\nbounds contain the pointer (or record, at hit-test time, whether any later\nsibling/ancestor-sibling overlaps the leaf and disable the short-circuit for\nit); and defer a coalesced move to the next frame instead of dropping it.","files":null}]}